1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,622
When you choose 30 FPS or 60 FPS in a game's
settings, what exactly are you changing?

2
00:00:06,622 --> 00:00:11,102
In a game, a frame is the result of drawing
the screen once: one image.

3
00:00:11,102 --> 00:00:15,460
Freeze any movement and you will find a
sequence of slightly different still images;

4
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:18,161
this video follows that single image into game
time,

5
00:00:18,161 --> 00:00:20,800
responsiveness, and modern graphics
technology.

6
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:27,452
FPS means frames per second: the frequency of
frames processed or displayed in one second.

7
00:00:27,452 --> 00:00:31,612
If the screen shows 60 images in one second,
we call it 60 FPS.

8
00:00:31,612 --> 00:00:34,519
In modern games, however, we sometimes need to
distinguish

9
00:00:34,519 --> 00:00:37,600
directly rendered frames from the frames
actually displayed.

10
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:41,462
Many early games moved a character a fixed
distance

11
00:00:41,462 --> 00:00:44,092
whenever the screen advanced by one frame.

12
00:00:44,092 --> 00:00:49,862
At frame zero, X is zero; on the next frame it
is one, and then two.

13
00:00:49,862 --> 00:00:52,603
A frame was both an image and a time marker,

14
00:00:52,603 --> 00:00:58,320
so a different refresh rate could also change
movement, animation, and physics speed.

15
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:03,022
Imagine the same character moving the same
distance during the same one second.

16
00:01:03,022 --> 00:01:05,869
At 30 FPS there are 30 position samples,

17
00:01:05,869 --> 00:01:10,612
while at 60 FPS there are 60, including more
positions in between.

18
00:01:10,612 --> 00:01:13,451
The destination and travel time are unchanged,

19
00:01:13,451 --> 00:01:18,560
but smaller steps let a higher frame rate show
the same time more densely and smoothly.

20
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:23,622
Why not simply make every game run at the
highest possible frame rate?

21
00:01:23,622 --> 00:01:28,812
At 30 FPS a frame has about 33.33
milliseconds;

22
00:01:28,812 --> 00:01:33,202
at 60 it has 16.67, and at 120 it has 8.33.

23
00:01:33,202 --> 00:01:37,278
The higher the frame rate, the smaller the
time budget for physics,

24
00:01:37,278 --> 00:01:39,680
animation, game logic, and rendering.

25
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:46,474
When more enemies, lights, and effects prevent
the work from finishing on time,

26
00:01:46,474 --> 00:01:49,012
a reading of 60 can fall to 50 or 40.

27
00:01:49,012 --> 00:01:52,623
Uneven intervals can feel rough even when
average FPS is high,

28
00:01:52,623 --> 00:01:56,720
so we also need to inspect frame time: how
long each image takes to make.

29
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,391
Modern games often keep game time advancing at
the

30
00:02:00,391 --> 00:02:03,482
same speed even when the display frame rate
changes.

31
00:02:03,482 --> 00:02:08,662
A common method is delta time: multiply speed
by the real time elapsed during a frame.

32
00:02:08,662 --> 00:02:14,222
Then 30-frame and 60-frame updates can reach
the same position after the same one second.

33
00:02:14,222 --> 00:02:18,679
Collision and physics may instead use a fixed
time step for stability; the key is not

34
00:02:18,679 --> 00:02:23,200
to treat display frequency and game-time
calculation as automatically identical.

35
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:28,942
PC games can also run at 120 or even 240 FPS.

36
00:02:28,942 --> 00:02:32,163
More intermediate frames can show fast motion
in finer steps

37
00:02:32,163 --> 00:02:35,572
and provide more chances to draw the result of
a new input sooner.

38
00:02:35,572 --> 00:02:39,363
But FPS is the number of frames produced by
the game, while hertz is the monitor's

39
00:02:39,363 --> 00:02:43,040
refresh frequency; they affect each other, but
they are not the same concept.

40
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:48,302
Older console games were strongly affected by
regional television standards.

41
00:02:48,302 --> 00:02:52,425
NTSC regions such as Japan and North America
used about 60 fields,

42
00:02:52,425 --> 00:02:55,572
while PAL regions in Europe and Australia used
50.

43
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:59,137
If game logic was tied to refresh and left
uncorrected,

44
00:02:59,137 --> 00:03:04,640
it could run at the 50-to-60 ratio; that was
the cost of using frames directly as time.

45
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:08,656
Many modern console games offer a choice
between

46
00:03:08,656 --> 00:03:12,032
image quality and frame rate on the same
hardware.

47
00:03:12,032 --> 00:03:16,704
On PlayStation 5, resolution or quality modes
generally favor higher image quality

48
00:03:16,704 --> 00:03:21,112
and complex effects, while performance modes
favor a higher target frame rate.

49
00:03:21,112 --> 00:03:24,008
Exact resolutions and targets vary by game,

50
00:03:24,008 --> 00:03:27,920
so quality is not always 30 and performance is
not always 60.

51
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:31,741
A frame can include geometry, lighting,
shadows,

52
00:03:31,741 --> 00:03:34,842
reflections, ray tracing, and post-processing.

53
00:03:34,842 --> 00:03:39,902
The frame budget does not grow when effects
are added, so the quality-versus-frame-rate

54
00:03:39,902 --> 00:03:43,680
tradeoff is a decision about where to spend
limited milliseconds.

55
00:03:44,400 --> 00:03:48,977
Graphics cards such as the RTX 50 Series
include hardware for ray tracing and AI

56
00:03:48,977 --> 00:03:53,622
computation, expanding the range of complex
lighting that can run in real time.

57
00:03:53,622 --> 00:03:58,139
Modern rendering can also reconstruct a final
screen from less computation instead

58
00:03:58,139 --> 00:04:02,720
of calculating every pixel and every frame
entirely through traditional rendering.

59
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:06,688
A 4K image contains about 8.29 million pixels,

60
00:04:06,688 --> 00:04:11,602
and ray tracing can require multiple ray
samples and additional work.

61
00:04:11,602 --> 00:04:14,162
As resolution and lighting costs rise,

62
00:04:14,162 --> 00:04:19,280
must every final pixel always be calculated
directly at 4K from the beginning?

63
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,672
DLSS Super Resolution renders below the target
output

64
00:04:23,672 --> 00:04:27,743
resolution and then constructs a
higher-resolution image.

65
00:04:27,743 --> 00:04:31,474
It is not simple enlargement: it uses multiple
lower-resolution frames,

66
00:04:31,474 --> 00:04:34,303
motion vectors, and information from previous
frames.

67
00:04:34,303 --> 00:04:37,308
The goal is to reduce directly calculated
pixels while

68
00:04:37,308 --> 00:04:40,053
restoring detail close to the output
resolution.

69
00:04:40,053 --> 00:04:42,245
This reconstructs pixels,

70
00:04:42,245 --> 00:04:47,680
which is a different job from Frame Generation
creating a new image.

71
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:51,531
Frame Generation analyzes motion between two
directly

72
00:04:51,531 --> 00:04:54,663
rendered frames and generates a new image
between them.

73
00:04:54,663 --> 00:05:00,823
On the timeline, directly rendered frames are
blue and generated frames are purple.

74
00:05:00,823 --> 00:05:04,251
Placing generated frames between rendered ones
can

75
00:05:04,251 --> 00:05:07,913
increase displayed frame count and motion
smoothness.

76
00:05:07,913 --> 00:05:11,814
But a generated frame is not a new simulation
of the game world,

77
00:05:11,814 --> 00:05:16,240
so base render speed and input latency do not
improve by the same ratio.

78
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:22,393
DLSS 4 introduced Multi Frame Generation on
the RTX 50 Series.

79
00:05:22,393 --> 00:05:25,959
At launch, it could generate up to three
additional

80
00:05:25,959 --> 00:05:28,863
frames for each directly rendered frame.

81
00:05:28,863 --> 00:05:32,676
DLSS 4.5 later expanded this direction with
dynamic

82
00:05:32,676 --> 00:05:36,403
adjustment and support for more generated
frames.

83
00:05:36,403 --> 00:05:39,240
Actual results depend on game support,

84
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:43,280
base render rate, resolution, GPU, and CPU
conditions.

85
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,306
Generated frames between directly rendered
frames can raise the displayed number,

86
00:05:49,306 --> 00:05:53,193
but not every image comes from a new input and
simulation step.

87
00:05:53,193 --> 00:05:56,414
That is why Rendered FPS and Displayed FPS
should be

88
00:05:56,414 --> 00:06:00,160
reported separately when interpreting Frame
Generation.

89
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:03,545
Input passes through game simulation and
rendering,

90
00:06:03,545 --> 00:06:07,573
then optionally upscaling and Frame
Generation, before appearing on a monitor.

91
00:06:07,573 --> 00:06:12,817
Rendered FPS is the direct rendering
frequency, Displayed FPS includes generated

92
00:06:12,817 --> 00:06:17,463
frames, and input latency is the time from a
button press to visible light.

93
00:06:17,463 --> 00:06:21,184
Technologies such as NVIDIA Reflex can reduce
system latency,

94
00:06:21,184 --> 00:06:23,853
but they do not merge these concepts into one.

95
00:06:23,853 --> 00:06:27,246
So inspect base render rate, frame time,
presentation method,

96
00:06:27,246 --> 00:06:30,000
and latency instead of relying on one large
number.

97
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:34,636
In many simple older games, game logic, frame
creation,

98
00:06:34,636 --> 00:06:38,053
and display output were closely tied in one
line.

99
00:06:38,053 --> 00:06:43,060
A modern game can separate input, simulation,
rendering, upscaling, Frame Generation,

100
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:47,200
and output, and those stages do not
necessarily run at the same frequency.

101
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:49,786
A frame is one screen image,

102
00:06:49,786 --> 00:06:54,653
and FPS is the frequency of frames processed
or displayed in one second.

103
00:06:54,653 --> 00:07:01,893
At 30, 60, and 120 FPS, the frame budgets are
roughly 33, 16, and 8 milliseconds.

104
00:07:01,893 --> 00:07:05,306
Delta time and fixed time steps help
game-world time

105
00:07:05,306 --> 00:07:08,953
advance consistently when display frequency
changes.

106
00:07:08,953 --> 00:07:11,907
Modern rendering requires us to distinguish
Super Resolution,

107
00:07:11,907 --> 00:07:15,793
directly rendered frames, generated frames,
displayed frames, and input latency.

108
00:07:15,793 --> 00:07:20,308
When building a game, choose a target frame
rate, measure frame time,

109
00:07:20,308 --> 00:07:25,680
and verify that game speed remains the same
across different performance levels.
